/*
  Wall clock: 28BYJ-48 + ULN2003 on ESP32  +  world-clock web page (single file)
  - Motor shaft turns the minute hand; the clock's own gears move the hour hand.
    1 motor turn = 1 hour, so going from 11:30 to 10:30 means a full round.
  - Hand position is saved in flash, so it is remembered after power off/reset.
  - Joins Wi-Fi and serves a page at http://wallclock.local (or the IP shown in
    Serial Monitor). Tap a city -> hands go to that city's time, then run normally.

  Serial Monitor (115200 baud):
    10:30   -> both hands move quickly to 10:30, then the clock runs normally
    c11:30  -> "the hands are showing 11:30 right now" (no movement, saved)
    ?       -> print the time the clock is showing and the web address
*/

#include <WiFi.h>
#include <WebServer.h>
#include <ESPmDNS.h>
